          "distinct": true,
          "id": "a0196e87c896a24d186800f2bc52ae6e5162afa4",
          "message": "feat(sparq-terse): verifiable LLM-ergonomic SPARQL transpiler + V() lexical-first concept resolution (sq-leg8n) [OPUS-4.8] (#1088)\n\n* feat(sparq-terse): verifiable LLM-ergonomic SPARQL transpiler + V() lexical-first concept resolution (sq-leg8n) [OPUS-4.8]\n\nNew OPT-IN crate `sparq-terse` (epic sq-2m6zm, design\nresearch/llm-ergonomic-sparql-surface.md §3/§6, PR #1074): a pre-parse\ntranspiler that ALWAYS emits canonical, conformant SPARQL — it never touches\nthe vendored spargebra grammar, so the engine only ever runs standard SPARQL\nthe agent can inspect.\n\nPhase 1 (default build, lean — only spargebra):\n- terse_to_sparql(src) -> Expansion { canonical_sparql, resolutions, warnings }\n- identity pass-through for canonical SPARQL (byte-identical), plus the\n  silent-rewrite canary (re-parse the emission under spargebra; a non-parsing\n  output is TerseError::CanaryFailed, never handed back).\n- a feature-independent V(\"phrase\") detector that fails LOUDLY\n  (TerseError::FeatureRequired) in the default build rather than passing an\n  un-expanded construct to the parser.\n\nPhase 2 (feature = \"vectors\"): V(\"phrase\") lexical-first concept resolution\nbehind the §6 soundness envelope:\n- lexical-FIRST via sparq-nlq's deterministic, no-model EntityLinker; the\n  staleness-guarded sparq-vectors search is a FALLBACK for fuzzy phrases.\n- every bind echoes IRI + score + runner-up + confidence + method.\n- confidence-gated: below the score floor or inside the ambiguity margin V()\n  refuses to bind (TerseError::Unresolved with candidates) — loud-fail beats\n  silent-wrong; never auto-accept the uncertain.\n- mandatory staleness guard: a store built against a different graph generation\n  is a hard TerseError::StaleStore (via VectorStore::check_graph).\n\nDesign decision (documented, flagged for the maintainer): the V() vector\nfallback takes a CALLER-supplied pre-embedded query vector — this crate never\nembeds free text itself, keeping the embedding model the caller's explicit\nopt-in dependency (design §6/§9 Q5). The lexical-only path needs no model.\n\nOpt-in / lean-core preserved: zero edits to sparq-core, sparq-engine, or the\nvendored parser; nothing in the default build depends on sparq-terse. Gated in\nBOTH feature states (build/clippy --all-targets -D warnings/test in default AND\nvectors), rustdoc --all-features -D warnings clean (workspace), readme-template\n0 deviations, privacy-claims + no-perf-numbers + typos clean. CI: two\nfeature-matrix legs (default + vectors). README + genai-retrieval SKILL.md\nupdated. Phases 3-6 (keyword layer, the §5 token/quality A/B verdict,\nserver/CLI exposure) remain tracked follow-ups.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(sparq-terse): unblock #1088 — default-build rustdoc links + feature-matrix default leg [OPUS-4.8]\n\n🤖 SPARQ agent — gate-green fixes for PR #1088 (sq-leg8n).\n\nTwo RED gates fixed:\n\n1. `clippy (gate)` (bundled rustdoc half): three intra-doc `[link]`s pointed at\n   `vectors`-gated items (`terse_to_sparql_with`, `crate::resolve::ResolveCtx`) from\n   doc comments that DO render in the DEFAULT build, so `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings\n   cargo doc --workspace --no-deps` errored with `rustdoc::broken-intra-doc-links`\n   (\"could not document sparq-terse\"). Demoted those broken links to plain inline\n   code spans (lib.rs:20, transpile.rs:70-71). The cfg(vectors)-gated doc comment on\n   `terse_to_sparql_with` itself keeps its working `[link]`s (resolve under\n   --all-features). Default AND --all-features rustdoc now clean.\n\n2. `opt-in sparq-terse (default …)`: the feature-matrix default leg had\n   `features: \"\"`, so the shared steps ran `cargo build -p sparq-terse --features `\n   (a bare `--features` with no value), which cargo rejects. Removed the redundant\n   default leg — the no-feature build is already covered by the workspace-wide\n   default lane in ci.yml (nextest --workspace --all-targets, clippy --workspace,\n   doc --workspace). Kept the `vectors` leg (the one that adds real coverage).\n\nVerified locally (default + --all-features): cargo doc -p sparq-terse clean under\n-D warnings; workspace doc clean both states; clippy --all-targets -D warnings clean;\n12 default unit tests + 8 vectors tests + doctests pass; YAML valid.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Wright <jmwright.045@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
